<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1935 · Egypt &amp; North Africa</p><h1 class="sg-title">Humat al-Hima</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ar" dir="rtl">حماة الحمى</bdi> / <bdi lang="ar-Latn">Ḥumāt al-Ḥimā</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The national anthem of Tunisia, made from a 1930s poem by an Egyptian poet and once sung as the &quot;Hymn of the Revolution&quot;.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Mostafa Saadeq Al-Rafe'ie; Aboul-Qacem Echebbi (the last verses)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>disputed: Mohammed Abdel Wahab, or, according to Salah El Mahdi, Ahmed Kheireddine; the poem's original music by Zakariyya Ahmad</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Tunisians; the Neo Destour party</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Egypt (the poem); Tunisia</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>poem written in the 1930s; Echebbi's verses added in June 1955; anthem 1957–1958 and since 1987</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words from a poem written in the 1930s by Mostafa Saadeq Al-Rafe'ie, with closing verses by Aboul-Qacem Echebbi (see notes))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/defenders-of-the-homeland">Defenders of the Homeland</a>&quot; (Arabic: Ḥumāt al-Ḥimā) is the national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. Its words come from a poem written in the 1930s by the Egyptian poet Mostafa Saadeq Al-Rafe'ie, and its last verses are by the Tunisian poet Aboul-Qacem Echebbi; according to the Tunisian musicologist Salah El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, those verses were added in June 1955 by the nationalist Mongi Slim. Who wrote the march's melody is disputed: some say Mohammed Abdel Wahab, while El Mahdi holds that it was the poet Ahmed Kheireddine, and that the poem's original music was by Zakariyya Ahmad.</p><p>Known as the &quot;Hymn of the Revolution&quot;, it was sung at the meetings of the ruling party, the Neo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/destour">Destour</a>, which later changed its name to the Socialist Destourian Party. When the monarchy ended on 25 July 1957 it replaced the &quot;Salam al-Bey&quot; and served as a provisional national anthem until 20 March 1958, when &quot;Ala Khallidi&quot; took its place. It was brought back after the coup d'état of 7 November 1987 that brought Zine El Abidine Ben Ali to power, and it has been the anthem since. On occasions that call for brevity a short version is sung: the chorus, the third verse once, and the chorus again.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A patriotic march addressed, as its title says, to the defenders of the homeland; a stirring, martial song, sung as the &quot;Hymn of the Revolution&quot; of the Neo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/destour">Destour</a> party before it became the anthem.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so they are not printed here. &#8220;What it says&#8221; above gives their theme in our own words, and the performance below lets you hear them.</p></section>
</div>
